WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:05.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.639
Testing modern computer games by using an
AI is getting more and more popular these

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:11.639
days.

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:17.930
This earlier work showcased how we can use
an automated agent test the integrity of the

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:22.170
game by finding spots where we can get stuck.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:27.840
And when we fixed the problem, we could easily
ask the agent to check whether the fix really

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.010
worked.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:33.660
In this case, it did!

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.730
And this new work also uses learning algorithms
to test our levels.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:44.550
Now this chap has been trained on a fixed
level, mastered it, and let’s see if it

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.109
has managed to obtain general knowledge from
it.

00:00:48.109 --> 00:00:49.109
How?

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:53.390
Well, by testing how it performs on a different
level.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:59.379
It is very confident, good…but..uh-oh!

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:02.510
As you see, it is confidently incorrect.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:09.160
So, is it possible to train an agent to be
able to beat these levels more reliably?

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.920
Well, how about creating a more elaborate
curriculum for them to learn on.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.340
Yes, let’s do that…but, with a twist!

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:25.230
In this work, the authors chose not to feed
the AI a fixed set of levels…no-no!

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:31.330
They created another AI that builds the levels
for the player AI.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:39.540
So, both the builder and the player are learning
algorithms, who are tasked to succeed together

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.850
in getting the agent to the finish line.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.120
They have to collaborate to succeed.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.990
Building the level means choosing the appropriate
distance, height, angle and size for these

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:51.990
blocks.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.180
Let’s see them playing together on an easy
level.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:03.730
Okay, so far so good, but let’s not let
them build a little cartel where only easy

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:07.790
levels are being generated so they get a higher
score.

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.310
I want to see a challenge!

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:15.859
To do that, let’s force the builder AI to
use a larger average distance between the

00:02:15.859 --> 00:02:20.499
blocks, thereby creating levels of a prescribed
difficulty.

00:02:20.499 --> 00:02:24.120
And with that, let’s ramp up the difficulty
a little.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.109
Things get a little more interesting here,
because… whoa!

00:02:28.109 --> 00:02:32.069
Do you see what I see here?

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:33.159
Look!

00:02:33.159 --> 00:02:38.050
It even found a shortcut to the end of the
level.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:42.799
And, let’s see the harder levels together.

00:02:42.799 --> 00:02:49.230
While many of these chaps failed, some of
them are still able to succeed.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.049
Very cool!

00:02:51.049 --> 00:02:57.500
Let’s compare the performance of the new
technique with the previous, fixed track agent.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.620
This is the chap that learned by mastering
only a fixed track.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.230
And this one learned in the wilderness.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.200
Neither of them have seen these levels before.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.389
So, who is going to be scrappier?

00:03:11.389 --> 00:03:16.180
Of course, the wilderness guy described in
the new technique.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.180
Excellent.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:22.450
So, all this sounds great, but I hear you
asking the key question here: what do we use

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:23.599
these for?

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:30.090
Well, one, the player AI can test the levels
that we are building for our game and give

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:37.480
us feedback on whether it is possible to finish,
is it too hard or too easy, and more.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:43.450
This can be a godsend when updating some levels,
because the agent will almost immediately

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:49.569
tell us whether it has gotten easier or harder,
or if we have broken the level.

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:51.959
No human testing is required.

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:58.279
Now, hold on to your papers, because the thing
runs so quickly that we can even refine a

00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:04.569
level in real time.

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:06.160
Loving it.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:12.010
Or two, the builder can also be given to a
human player who might enjoy a level being

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:15.950
built in real time in front of them.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.000
And here comes the best part.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.560
The whole concept generalizes well for other
kinds of games too.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:29.729
Look, the builder can build race tracks, and
the player can try to drive through them.

00:04:29.729 --> 00:04:35.110
So, do these great results also generalize
to the racing game?

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.120
Let’s see what the numbers say.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:43.840
The agent that trained on a fixed track can
succeed on an easy level about 75% of the

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:50.639
time, while the newly proposed agent can do
it nearly with a 100% chance.

00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:53.210
A bit of an improvement, okay.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.180
Now, look at this.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:02.460
The fixed track agent can only beat a hard
level about 2 times out of 10, while the new

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.669
agent can do it about six times out of ten.

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:08.599
That is quite a bit of an improvement.

00:05:08.599 --> 00:05:14.780
Now, note that in a research paper, choosing
a proper baseline to compare to is always

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:15.900
a crucial question.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:21.409
I would like to note that the baseline here
is not the state of the art, and with that,

00:05:21.409 --> 00:05:25.620
it is a little easier to make the new solution
pop.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.580
No matter, the solutions are still good, but
I think this is worth a note.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:37.009
So, from now on, whenever we create a new
level in a computer game, we can have hundreds

00:05:37.009 --> 00:06:11.789
of competent AI players testing it in real
time.

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:14.210
So good!

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:39.430
What a time to 
be alive!

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:49.029
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

